    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7b9",
      "function_role": "dominant-substitute-reframing",
      "narrative": "Baker teaches that 'they aren't diminished chords at all when used in this respect \u2014 they are the Upper tones to a diminished or flatted 9th chord.' A G7b9 with root omitted is presented side-by-side with B diminished (Exercises 51a and 51b) to prove harmonic equivalence: the dom7b9 shell is the conceptual parent, and the dim7 is its rootless upper structure. Chapter 21, p. 34.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "mickey-baker:complete-course-vol-2:functional-diatonic-harmony:diminished-as-dominant-substitute"
