      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "upper-partials-ii-chord-extension-policy",
      "narrative": "Baker prescribes that the II chord \u2014 like the dominant \u2014 carries its own Upper Partials: 'With the II chord of the scale the same principal applies, you have the 6th, 7th, 9th, 11th, and 13th. Any of these forms of the II chord can be used to resolve into the V which in turn resolves into the tonic.' The min7 is the base; min9, min11, min13, and min6 are all valid superstructures for the II-chord role. Chapter 10, p. 14.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "mickey-baker:complete-course-vol-2:functional-diatonic-harmony:upper-partials-dominant-alteration"
