    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "dominant-substitute-upper-structure",
      "narrative": "Baker teaches that diminished 7th chords functioning as dominants 'aren't diminished chords at all \u2026 they are the Upper tones to a diminished or flatted 9th chord.' He presents G7b9 with root omitted (Ex. 51a) beside B diminished (Ex. 51b) and instructs students to 'analyze the two chords' \u2014 proving that the dim7 is a rootless dom7b9. Chapter 21, p. 34.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "mickey-baker:complete-course-vol-2:functional-diatonic-harmony:diminished-as-dominant-substitute"
